“You are brave, Selina. Rushing in to help a teammate is a quality not many people here have.”
Selina:
“You don’t need to make me feel better… I need to lie down.”
I move to a more open area and lie back. The floor isn’t very comfortable, but it will do.
My backpack and gear disconnect with a simple press, and I lay them next to me.
The drone’s noises give a nice, albeit weird, ambiance. There’s a slight rhythm to it.
I want to sleep this away. But before I can close my eyes, Alice makes her way over.
Alice:
“So much for an easy assignment huh? Sorry about the false expectations.”
Selina:
“Alice, why are you apologizing?”
“I’m not meant to be here. As nice as it is to see you again, I can’t stay here. I’m going to get people killed, and I’m not ready for it.”
Alice:
“I get it. It isn’t an easy job, throwing you in the deep end is so cruel.”
“I can’t make you stay, but if you leave, our team will be short a person till the next season.”
“And to be frank, I want you here with us.”
“This planet has more than just bugs and mining, there are some really pretty locations I want you to see.”
Selina:
“I believe you, but what’s the point if we die getting there?”
Alice:
“Selina, that is an inherent risk in everything we do. What’s the point of just focusing on that.”
“Our job is to explore uncharted areas and be the prospectors on this beautiful planet.”
“I hate the company for scamming me out of my geology job, but this is still a great opportunity.”
“Speaking of which, mission control.”
She shouts something at Ben, the echo of the cave makes her words incoherent to me.
Alice:
“He’ll deal with that for now.”
Selina:
“No matter what, I think those higher-ups don’t want me with them. This whole mission control thing suggests they are deliberately trying to kill me.”
Alice disconnects her gear and lies down with a heavy thud.
Alice:
“I still think they have something bigger planned for you, and it might just be a test.”
“Equipping you with a new class? It might just be an R&D thing.”
“Selina, please.”
“You took care of me all those years ago, let me take care of you this time.”
Selina:
“...”
She’s probably right. The whole caving thing is pretty interesting. It reminds me of this game I played as a kid.
But back then, it was just a game and dying was just a punishment to retry. 
In real life, one wrong step, one bad choice… I don’t want to think about every possible outcome.
Being a little more careful is not too much to ask for, I suppose.
Selina:
“Fine, depending on how this assignment ends, I might stick around.”
Alice:
“I promise you, it’s not usually this scary.”
She takes my hand and gives it an affirming squeeze.
Selina:
“...”
Hope you’re right, Alice.
…
The buzzing of the drone fills the silence.
…
I’m kind of hungry. 
I give Alice’s hand one more rub before sitting up.
Selina:
“What food did they pack us? I could go for some tofu.”
Alice:
“They prepare the food by species, it’s actually pretty good.”
“Guys, come on, let's eat together.”
She waves the other two over, and we start unpacking our food.
…
…
Another night of leftover dinner.
As unappetizing as this is, I should really eat up.
The appointment is tomorrow.
I’m finally getting rid of these large horns.
A pain in the ass my entire life, I don’t get how others live like this.
Deers shed their antlers annually, but our stupid horns are here for life.
Lucky bastards. 
My parents kept trying to convince me not to prune it. 
I don’t care if it's permanent. I want it out of the way.
I’m a grown adult now, you live off my paychecks, so you don’t get any say in this.
If you hadn’t had me so late, we wouldn’t have to depend on just me to get by.
We’ve had this argument so many times. Why can’t you guys just appreciate something?
All this is just about my stupid horns. Why is this the thing you care so much about?
Ugh.
Just thinking about having small horns though…
I feel a little better—just enough motivation.
I start eating my food.
…
I’ll be honest, the food was just ok, but I’ve never eaten MREs before so I have no frame of reference.
It’s kind of awkward being the only herbivore in the group, I couldn’t participate in trying out other people’s meals.
Alice did give me some of her vegetables though, it was alright.
She also managed to get Ben to talk more about himself.
Sucks what’s happening to his home, not being able to be there for your friends must be tough.
That must be pretty hard on him, so I think I can excuse his attitude for the time being.
I’ve learned about their previous planet-wide disaster, the one back in 2234, a year before I was born.
Apparently, no one knows why it happened then, and no one knows why it’s happening now.
I think Silacore was the one attributed to solving the first crisis. Crazy considering they have nothing related.
This brings up the question of if Silacore will do it again, and if Ben being here is just a coincidence.
Maybe he owes the company something? Who knows.
I didn’t get the chance to ask him anything else because mission control jutted in.
Mission control:
“Team Psi, listen up.”
“The station’s communication bandwidth has been exceeded due to unknown reasons.”
“We are attempting to fix it as we speak. Until then, communication between teams and the station will have to enter queue mode.”
“I have four other teams to get to, stay sharp.”
Alice:
“Hey hey don’t just leave us like that.”
“MC, what do you mean by queue mode?”
“... Hello?”
Felix:
“I don’t want to think about other teams who would have had it worse than us.”
Alice:
“Aw nooo… Is this something I’m going to have to deal with?”
“Ohhh… I’m really hoping there aren’t any other incidents because of this.”
“Damn it. Let’s wrap this assignment up. I need to make it back before any commotion starts.”
Right, Alice is a leader of the union. Honestly, I can’t see her manage to organize hundreds of angry men.
But to be fair, I haven’t met anyone else yet, if they all have the temperament of Felix, it might not be so hard.
If we were to believe MC’s explanation, I guess it kind of discredits the theory they are trying to get me killed by just a little.
Ben:
“Wonder if my messages even made it to them at all.”
Selina:
“I’m hoping this isn’t a normal occurrence.”
Felix sighs, as if he’s about to shatter my world.
Felix:
“This has occurred before. Unfortunately.”
“Just before I joined, there were words of a communication problem. There was a big commotion too I believe, but it was solved rather quickly.”
“I did not think that was a real concern at the time. But there must be some extreme flaw aboard the station for this to happen twice.”
Selina:
“What did I get myself into…”
Ben:
“Alice.”
“You need to figure this out.”
Alice:
“It is my job, ay?”
Ben:
“They need to pay for what they’re doing to us.”
“If any other workers are as much as injured because of this, this company has to be put in the ground.”
“I don’t believe for a moment this is a spontaneous issue.”
Alice:
“That’s a hard ask, I’m not really the person to be investigating this thing.”
Selina:
“As much as I don’t want to believe there’s malice, I do agree, this problem should not be happening to a company this large.”
I see Ben starting to furiously type on his wrist.
Felix:
“Yes. Exactly my idea.”
Ben:
“Sorry to rain on your parade, lady, those people are evil.”
“You can’t be this naive.”
Selina:
“Excuse me?”
“What is your problem?”
Ben:
“There are going to be people dying because of this…”
Selina:
“We don’t know that.”
Ben:
“...and you don’t believe that this is just a ploy to thin the numbers?”
Alice:
“Oy oy oy, enough of that, let’s fucking move on.”
Selina:
“Dude, I obviously don’t want to believe that conspiracy.”
Ben:
“Look what they did to me. No matter I do, they won’t fix me.”
Luke smacks Ben’s back, and his hand slips off the wrist keyboard.
Ben:
“They have the technology to reconstruct matter out of rocks, and they wo-khgfsxz”
Felix:
“I’m going to stop you here, Ben.”
“Arguing here won’t change anything. Like Alice said, we should move on so we can contact the other teams.”
Ben turns and walks further into the cave, but not before giving Luke an angry side eye. 
Luke breathes a heavy sigh and goes to get the drone started on working on the minerals we mined out.
Seeing nowhere else to go, I follow Ben’s direction deeper into the cave.
The rest of the team follows. 
…
Alice starts walking alongside me.
Alice:
“...”
“When did you learn how to shoot?”
Selina:
“Huh?”
Alice:
“When did you learn to shoot like that? Your accuracy was good back there.”
Selina:
“It’s… I did airsoft in college. That’s really about it.”
“You might have heard about that phase, I attended tournaments and such too.”
Alice:
“I don’t think I did. But anyhow, that’s cool. How does it feel to shoot a real gun?”
I take out my gun, it’s still a bit warm from the battle earlier.
Selina:
“It’s not what I expected. This thing is like a coilgun rather than a normal gun.”
Alice:
“Yeah? Let me try it.”
Selina:
“Sure. Uhm, how do I…”
It’s still attached to my belt, there’s just about enough cord length for it to be shouldered by me. 
Uhm… I just hand the gun to Alice. She awkwardly grabs it and tries to properly shoulder it, but the cord is fully taut. 
Alice gives it a firm tug, pulling me a little closer.
Alice:
“Is that as long as it gets?”
She turns and backs up right in front of me. Her feathers nearly poking me. 
Alice:
“Good ergonomics on this thing.”
She points the gun around, tugging on me every time she turns.
Alice:
“Cool.”
“Kind of jealous they gave you such a cool gun.”
She turns to her left and the cord yanks me into her.
She gives me a playful look.
Alice:
“Oops.”
Selina:
“Alice! Watch it!”
“I don’t want to get my stuff broken on the first day. Give me it back.”
Alice keeps her playful expression for a second as if waiting for something. She then drops the act and hands me my gun.
Alice:
“Chill out. Here.”
She pouts a little.

